diff --git a/docs/business-rules.md b/docs/business-rules.md
index 7a3126a..b6e6b2d 100644
--- a/docs/business-rules.md
+++ b/docs/business-rules.md
@@ -76,6 +76,16 @@ ### Ticket
 - Issuance timestamp (ordering "oldest first" within same priority).
 - Issuance channel: totem, virtual, printed copy, etc.
 
+**Queue.** Belongs to one entity. Has a `name`, a `slug` unique within the entity, an `is_active` flag (default: active) and optional `labels_i18n`. An inactive queue accepts no new tickets.
+
+**Issuance.** A new ticket is `waiting`, with `issued_at` = now, channel `virtual` and priority `0` unless given. A negative priority is rejected. Issuance rejects an inactive queue: the state is read from the database under a row lock, so a queue deactivated after the caller loaded it is still rejected. Nothing is persisted on rejection. A unique `(queue, code)` collision during issuance is retried a few times.
+
+**Display code.** A per-queue sequence `A001`, `A002`, …, zero-padded to at least three digits; beyond 999 it grows (`A1000`, `A1001`). The next code is the last valid code by insertion order, plus one (so `A999` followed by `A1000` yields `A1001`). Codes not matching `A<digits>` are skipped. Deleting a ticket does not reuse its number while a later valid ticket exists (after A001..A003 with A002 deleted, the next is A004). Deleting the most recent valid ticket makes its number the next one again.
+
+**Write-once.** After issuance only `status`, `called_at`, `called_by_user_id` (and `updated_at`) may change; any other change is rejected. A called ticket cannot return to `waiting`.
+
+**Call.** A ticket is called exactly once: it becomes `called` and records `called_at` and the calling user. Only members of the ticket's entity may call it; anyone else is refused and the ticket is unchanged. Calling a ticket that is not `waiting` is refused. Choosing *which* ticket is called next (priority and fairness) is not implemented yet.
+
 ### Priority
 
 Priority is an **integer**: the **higher the value, the more prioritized**. Named levels are configurable presets per entity that map to values; ordering uses the number, not the label.
